antisymmetric-wavefunction-two-fermions.limits_and_boundary:
The primary implication of the antisymmetric wavefunction for two identical fermions is not typically analyzed in terms of asymptotic limits of the wavefunction itself, but rather in terms of the exclusion principle it enforces. For example, as the separation between two identical fermions approaches zero (
|\mathbf{r}$_1 - \mathbf${r}_2| \to 0
), the antisymmetric wavefunction must approach zero if their spins are such that they would occupy the same state (e.g., both spin-up). This prevents the particles from becoming arbitrarily close in the same quantum state. Conversely, as the separation between the fermions becomes very large (
|\mathbf{r}$_1 - \mathbf${r}_2| \to \infty
), the system can be approximated as two independent, non-interacting fermions. In this limit, the total wavefunction can often be factored into single-particle wavefunctions, while still respecting the overall antisymmetry requirement, for instance, by forming a Slater determinant.
